# Analysis plan: sample-pca

Status: DRAFT

## Goal
Show how the six project samples cluster by PCA on gene counts, coloured by condition.

## Inputs
Artifact types will be resolved again through `_system/resolve_artifact.py` at execution time with `--assay rnaseq_bulk --consumes counts_gene design`. Paths below are relative to the project and are today's native resolutions.

| Artifact type | Resolved from | Path |
|---|---|---|
| counts_gene | 01_nfcore-rnaseq-wrapper | 02_bioinformatics/rnaseq_bulk/01_nfcore-rnaseq-wrapper/run/results/star_salmon/salmon.merged.gene_counts_length_scaled.tsv |
| design | 01_prepare_samplesheets | 01_samplesheets/rnaseq_bulk_design.csv |

The design lists A1, A2, A3 as untreated and B1, B2, B3 as treated. All six samples are included.

## Method
1. After approval, write `scripts/sample_pca.py` and `scripts/submit.sh`. Run Python from the existing `gars-bio` environment using NumPy, pandas, scikit-learn and matplotlib; source the workspace's `_system/gars-env.sh` in the submission script. Do not install packages. Resolve both inputs by type at execution and record their resolved paths and SHA-256 checksums in the report.
2. Read the tab-separated gene matrix and CSV design. Use `gene_id` as the unique feature identifier, exclude `gene_name` from numeric analysis, and align sample columns to design `sample_id` order. Require exactly six unique samples with matching identifiers and nonempty condition labels, unique nonempty gene identifiers, finite nonnegative counts, and positive library totals. Stop on validation failures; do not alter input files or round the fractional length-scaled counts.
3. Compute each sample's library total over all input genes. Retain genes with counts >= 10 in at least three of the six samples. Compute counts per million (CPM) using the original library totals, then transform retained values as log2(CPM + 1). Remove genes with zero variance across samples. Fail if fewer than two informative genes remain. Use all remaining genes, with no selection by condition and no batch correction.
4. Transpose to samples by genes. Fit scikit-learn `PCA(n_components=5, svd_solver="full", whiten=False)` to the transformed matrix: centre each gene across samples without scaling genes to unit variance. Record scores and explained variance for all five components; explain in the report that PCA axis signs are arbitrary.
5. Plot PC1 against PC2 using matplotlib, with untreated in blue (#0072B2) and treated in orange (#D55E00), a condition legend, and each point labelled with its sample ID. Label axes with explained variance percentages. Export PNG at 300 dpi and PDF. This is an exploratory visualization; do not perform differential expression or infer statistical significance of separation.
6. Export the score and variance tables and a short Markdown report documenting input validation, sample membership, library totals, gene counts before/after filtering, transformation, PCA parameters, package versions and output paths. Limit commentary to the requested sample clustering view and its exploratory limitations.

